digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56797-8
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) GTPases are essential regulatory proteins that are highly conserved across species."];
  "N11" [label="(7,0,0) OLA1/YchF is involved in various cellular stress response pathways."];
  "N12" [label="(8,0,0) OLA1 is implicated in the regulation of tumor progression in numerous types of cancer."];
  "N13" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: OLA1 regulates stress and cancer phenotypes."];
  "N14" [label="(14,0,0) Growing evidence shows that OLA1/YchF plays important roles in translation regulation."];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: OLA1 links stress/cancer to translation regulation."];
  "N16" [label="(18,0,0) Ribosome binding of OLA1/YchF has been observed in E.coli, yeast, Trypanosoma, plants, and human cells."];
  "N17" [label="(17,0,0) A recent study in Mollicutes found that YchF is one of the core set proteins that sustain ribosome biogenesis."];
  "N18" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: OLA1 is a universally conserved ribosome-binding protein."];
  "N19" [label="(19,0,0) The available data suggest that YchF/OLA1 binds not only to fully assembled ribosomes but also independently to subunits."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) They play crucial roles in regulating various biological processes."];
  "N20" [label="(20,0,0) Moreover, the 70S ribosome has been reported to stimulate the ATPase activity of YchF."];
  "N22" [label="(25,0,0) Disruption in translation can be caused by truncated mRNA or mRNA with specific structures/sequences."];
  "N23" [label="(26,0,0) Rescuing stalled ribosomes on these mRNAs requires numerous ribosome-associated proteins."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Stalled ribosomes need specific rescue factors."];
  "N25" [label="(28,0,0) Stalls on specific mRNA structures/sequences can be resolved under optimal conditions, i.e., synthesis of P-rich is made possible by EF-P."];
  "N26" [label="(29,0,0) Stalling on R/K-rich mRNA is promoted by Rbg1/Tma46 complex or targeted/split by the RQT complex."];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Specific mRNA sequences require specific rescue factors."];
  "N28" [label="(32,0,0) Currently, there is limited research on D/E-rich mRNA."];
  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: GTPases are essential regulators of biological processes."];
  "N30" [label="(22,0,0) Questions remain unanswered regarding the molecular details of ribosome binding and translation regulation."];
  "N31" [label="(23,0,0) One major reason for this gap is the lack of structural data capturing OLA1/YchF in complex with ribosomes."];
  "N33" [label="(33,0,0) Through structural analysis and Ribo-seq data, we speculate that OLA1/YchF enhances translation on D/E-rich mRNA."];
  "N35" [label="(31,0,0) The ATP hydrolysis-coupled structural rearrangement is critical for handling ribosome stalling caused by nascent peptides."];
  "N36" [label="(34,0,0) OLA1/YchF binds to the large subunit at the uL14/bL19 position via its helical domain, preventing the re-association of split ribosomes."];
  "N38" [label="(24,0,0) Here, we report that OLA1/YchF binds to the ribosomal large subunit and promotes efficient translation on D/E-rich mRNA."];
  "N39" [label="(35,0,0) These findings not only provide deeper insights into OLA1/YchF function but also contribute to a better understanding of translation regulation on D/E-rich mRNA."];
  "N4" [label="(3,0,0) GTPases can be divided into two large superclasses: SIMIBI and TRAFAC."];
  "N5" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: TRAFAC superclass includes translation factors."];
  "N6" [label="(4,0,0) OLA1 and its bacteria homolog YchF belong to the TRAFAC class, Obg family, and YchF subfamily."];
  "N7" [label="(5,0,0) The OLA1/YchF proteins possess a core ATPase domain and are ubiquitously expressed."];
  "N8" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: OLA1 is a conserved TRAFAC ATPase."];
  "N9" [label="(6,0,0) The G4 motif in the YchF subfamily leads to members losing GTP specificity and preferentially hydrolyzing ATP."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Research proposal: determine how the conserved OLA1/YchF ATPase regulates translation of D/E-rich mRNAs during protein synthesis, testing whether its helical-domain interaction with the uL14/bL19 region of the ribosomal large subunit and its effects on split-ribosome re-association contribute to relief of stalling.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A conserved ribosome-associated ATPase that affects ribosomal subunit behavior and is linked to sequence-dependent translation can be studied as a mechanistic regulator of stalling during protein synthesis."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: D/E-rich mRNA translation is an underexplored sequence-dependent translation context in which ribosome-associated regulatory factors may be worth investigating."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: The ribosome-associated ATPase context of OLA1/YchF provides biochemical background for investigating, but not validating, the speculation that OLA1/YchF enhances translation on D/E-rich mRNA."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: The reported helical-domain binding of OLA1/YchF to the uL14/bL19 position on the ribosomal large subunit, together with its prevention of split-ribosome re-association, is a specific structural observation relevant to mechanisms that handle ribosome stalling."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: The reported finding that OLA1/YchF binds the ribosomal large subunit and promotes efficient translation on D/E-rich mRNA provides evidence contributing to the study of translation regulation on D/E-rich mRNA."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: The structural observation involving OLA1/YchF, the ribosomal large subunit, and split-ribosome re-association offers a plausible mechanistic explanation to test for the reported speculation that OLA1/YchF enhances D/E-rich mRNA translation."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: The functional report on D/E-rich mRNA translation and the plausible structural mechanism together motivate a research proposal on how OLA1/YchF regulates sequence-dependent translation."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Because molecular details of ribosome binding and translation regulation remain unresolved and structural data of OLA1/YchF-ribosome complexes are lacking, additional structural data are needed to clarify the mechanism."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Given conserved ribosome binding by OLA1/YchF and structural/Ribo-seq-based speculation that it enhances translation on D/E-rich mRNA, OLA1/YchF is a plausible candidate regulator of D/E-rich mRNA translation."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Because OLA1/YchF is a conserved TRAFAC ATPase and the YchF G4-motif variant is associated with reduced GTP specificity and preferential ATP hydrolysis, OLA1/YchF is best described as ATPase-biased rather than exclusively GTP-specific."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Available ribosome-binding data and reported 70S stimulation of YchF ATPase activity support a ribosome-associated ATPase context for OLA1/YchF, without proving that all ribosome binding directly activates OLA1."];
  "N1" -> "N3" [label="induction-case"];
